  "query_preview": "..\nIssued 1250 AM CDT Thu Aug 13 2026\n\nSaturday through Wednesday:\n\nSynopsis: Tied to a surface front lingering just south of southern \nWisconsin, leftover thunderstorms may be ongoing across parts of the \nregion on Saturday morning/the beginning of the long term period. \nThe responsible boundary will attempt to work north/into southern \nWisconsin as a warm front on Saturday afternoon, with its ultimate \nprogress being dictated by lingering clouds & precipitation. An \nupper wave will swing through the Great Lakes Saturday night into \nSunday, pushing the boundary back southward as a cold front. With \nthe front in the vicinity & the aforementioned wave moving through, \nshower and thunderstorm chances will thus continue Saturday night \ninto perhaps Sunday. The surface front will shift south of the \nregion by Sunday evening, bringing a noticeably drier air mass into \nthe area for Monday. Moisture will gradually work back into southern \nWisconsin Tuesday through Wednesday, bringing returning shower and \nthunderstorm chances along with it. \n\nSaturday Night through Sunday: Anticipating at least one window of \nshower/storm potential as a surface front moves through southern \nWisconsin. Most global guidance points to a Saturday overnight \nfrontal passage, which would combine with ascent from the passing \nmid-level wave to support the best overall window of opportunity for \nthunderstorms. Have thus maintained precip probabilities from the \nNBM, which reach their highest values Saturday night/during the \npassage of the overlapping forcing mechanisms. Depending on the \nfront's forward speed, could then see some additional development on \nSunday afternoon. Details regarding any severe storm potential \n(particularly Saturday evening/night) remain murky at this time, \nwith lingering clouds/precip on Saturday afternoon being the key \ninfluence on the amount of instability that can build back into the \narea with the warm front. Will be monitoring trends & providing \nupdates over coming forecasts.  \n\nQuigley",
